Suppose $g_1 H = g'_1 H$ and $g_2 H = g'_2 H$. Since $H nsub G$, the second equality also gives $H g_2 = H g'_2$, so $g_1 = g'_1 h_1$ and $g_2 = h_2 g'_2$ for some $h_1, h_2 in H$. Thus $g_1 g_2 = g'_1 (h_1 h_2) g'_2 = g'_1 g'_2 h_3$ for some $h_3 in H$, and the operation is well-defined.

Associativity follows from associativity in $G$. The identity is $e H = H$, the inverse of $g H$ is $g^(-1) H$, and closure follows from the definition of the operation. Hence $G \/ H$ is a group.
